          "Cross-shard transactions spanning independent storage partitions (for instance debiting Vault A on shard-1 while crediting Vault B on shard-2) require all-or-nothing atomicity: either both shards commit permanently, or both roll back cleanly.",
          "Classic Two-Phase Commit (2PC) splits coordination into a strict voting phase (`PREPARE`) followed by a durable resolution phase (`COMMIT` or `ABORT`). Before issuing votes, every step writes an explicit synchronous record to local write-ahead logs (`fsync`).",
          "During Phase 1, the Coordinator logs `TX_START(tx_id, cohorts)` locally and sends `PREPARE` to every cohort. Each participant acquires exclusive locks, validates balance constraints, writes an immutable undo/redo `PREPARED` log record to durable disk, and votes `YES` or `NO`.",
          "Voting `YES` binds a participant irrevocably until Phase 2 resolves: once cohort C replies `YES`, it surrenders local autonomy to decide unilaterally—it promises it CAN and WILL commit if told, OR abort if told, even after power loss!",
          "During Phase 2, if ALL cohorts voted `YES`, the Coordinator MUST log `DECISION: COMMIT` durably BEFORE informing any cohort! That single synchronous coordinator disk write marks the irrevocable point of no return.",
          "Conversely, if ANY cohort votes `NO` or times out during Phase 1, the Coordinator logs `DECISION: ABORT` (under Presumed Abort optimizations even skipping synchronous abort logs safely!) and dispatches `ABORT` to every prepared cohort.",
          "Atomic transactions spanning independent shards or microservices cannot rely on a single local database WAL because each node fails and restarts independently. If Shard A commits an inventory debit while Shard B aborts the corresponding payment credit, business balances diverge permanently. Two-Phase Commit (`2PC`) solves cross-shard atomicity by splitting execution into a voting `prepare` phase and a binding `finish` decision phase.",
          "In the prepare phase, the coordinator sends `Prepare` to every `Participant` in the transaction cohort. Each `Participant.prepare` acquires exclusive write locks, validates integrity constraints, forces a `PREPARED` log record to durable storage via `fsync`, and returns either `VOTE_COMMIT` or `VOTE_ABORT`. Voting `VOTE_COMMIT` surrenders local unilateral abort authority: the participant must stay ready to either commit or roll back later.",
          "If any single participant replies `VOTE_ABORT` or fails to respond before the coordinator's prepare timeout, the coordinator records `ABORT` and instructs all cohorts to release locks in Phase 2. Only when every single participant votes `VOTE_COMMIT` does the coordinator append `COMMIT` to `coord_wal`—the irrevocable commit point of the entire distributed transaction.",
          "Durability ordering inside `coord_wal` defines the narrow failure boundary of `2PC`. Before sending `COMMIT` messages to participants, the coordinator must force `COMMIT` to disk; once written, even an immediate coordinator crash recovers the decision and drives every participant to commit. Conversely, crashing before writing `COMMIT` allows recovery to choose `ABORT` cleanly because no participant received a commit order.",
          "Participants execute `finish(tx_id, decision)` in Phase 2 by appending `COMMIT` or `ABORT` to their local WAL, applying or undoing buffered mutations, and releasing held row locks. Releasing locks before logging `ABORT` or `COMMIT` risks dirty reads or lost updates if a restart replays earlier uncommitted operations. Writing the local decision first guarantees idempotent convergence even under duplicate Phase 2 retries.",
          "Notice the asymmetric failure consequence created by `VOTE_COMMIT`: once a participant writes `PREPARED` to disk, it enters the uncertain in-doubt window. Local operators cannot unilaterally force a commit or rollback without risking split decisions across other shards. Long-lived prepared locks stall unrelated queries touching those rows until the coordinator recovers or answers status polls.",
          "Engineering production transactional routers over `Participant` cohorts highlights why `2PC` trades high availability for strict atomicity. Whenever cross-datacenter latency exceeds tens of milliseconds, holding Phase 1 locks across WAN round-trips caps maximum transaction throughput sharply. Understanding that exact blocking boundary prepares teams to evaluate coordinator recovery, lock contention, and eventual-consistency alternatives accurately. Explicit two-phase WAL durability protects transaction outcomes cleanly across arbitrary power losses.",
