          "Production LSM engines choose between two classical compaction strategies trading off write amplification versus space and read efficiency: **Size-Tiered Compaction (STCS)** vs **Leveled Compaction (LCS)**. Size-Tiered groups similarly sized SSTables together (popular in Cassandra write-heavy tables), keeping write amplification low (`~10x`) at the cost of temporarily requiring up to 50% spare free disk during massive top-tier merges and higher read fan-out.",
          "**Leveled Compaction** (default in LevelDB and RocksDB) enforces strict size caps per level (`L1 = 10x`, `L2 = 100x`, `L3 = 1000x` base) and partitions each level `L >= 1` into **non-overlapping key-range SSTables**. Because keys inside L1, L2, and L3 never overlap within the same level, reading any key inspects at most *one file per level >= 1*, cutting read amplification and steady-state space waste down under 10%, while accepting higher write amplification (`~10x–30x`) as upper files merge repeatedly downward.",
