          "Recognize why **virtual nodes (`vnodes`)** remain essential alongside bounded loads on hash rings: placing just 1 physical point per server on `[0, 2**32)` creates huge gaps where one unlucky node owns 3x as much keyspace as its neighbor even before hot keys hit! Assigning `100–256` deterministic vnode tokens per physical host smooths baseline key distribution variance down under a few percent—while automatically allocating proportional weight to larger hardware tiers.",
          "Checking spillover cleanup (`release` / sliding load counters) matters whenever `BoundedHashRing` routes transient stateful streams vs long-lived storage caches. For in-memory video caches or stateless RPC routing, active connection/request counters naturally decay as streams end (`load -= 1`), letting spillover nodes cool back smoothly once viral surges pass. Note that persistent database writes rarely spill randomly without losing key lookup determinism unless bounded replication tracks placement metadata.",
          "Running `BoundedHashRing.pick_node` against extreme Zipfian traffic skew demonstrates balanced CPU utilization across every node instantly without losing cache stickiness for cold keys. Consistent hashing plus bounded spillover cleanly eliminates the classic hot-shard bottleneck in horizontally scaled fleets.",
