          "Circuit breakers protect overloaded shards from futile timeout waits when upstream callers keep hammering a crashed or partitioned database, yet naive fixed-count breakers (`trip after 5 errors`) cause disaster on high-QPS traffic! On a server handling 10,000 QPS, a tiny 0.05% background glitch produces 5 transient timeouts in milliseconds and falsely **trips the circuit breaker across 100% of traffic (`self-inflicted outage`)**! Conversely, on a low-QPS shard (2 QPS), waiting for 100 cumulative errors lets callers hang for fifty seconds before breaking!",
          "`should_trip_shard_breaker(sample_count, error_count, min_samples, error_rate_threshold)` implements the exact statistical gating pattern popularized by **Hystrix / Resilience4j / Envoy**: evaluating error ratio ONLY once statistical sample volume reaches `min_samples`! Below `min_samples`, transient single-packet drops never panic-trip an entire shard offline; once `sample_count >= min_samples` AND `error_count / sample_count >= error_rate_threshold`, the breaker snaps open cleanly (`OPEN`), returning fast local errors while letting the damaged shard heal.",
          "Follow the complete three-state **Circuit Breaker state machine** (`CLOSED -> OPEN -> HALF_OPEN -> CLOSED`): during healthy operation (`CLOSED`), traffic flows normally across rolling sample windows. Once `should_trip_shard_breaker` trips to `OPEN`, requests fail fast (`0 ms` thread blocking!) for a cooldown sleep (`open_duration_ms`). Afterward, the breaker steps cautiously into `HALF_OPEN`—admitting strictly a tiny probe budget (`e.g., 1–5 canary requests`) rather than unleashing a full thundering flood!",
