          "Collecting and storing 100% of tracing spans in high-volume production systems (`100,000+ QPS * 30 spans/request = 3 million spans/sec`) costs more infrastructure bandwidth and storage money than the entire application cluster itself! Traditional **Head-Based Sampling** (picking `sampled = 01` randomly on `1%` of requests right at the API edge) keeps costs low, yet fails catastrophically on rare production incidents: by the time a backend payment shard throws an unexpected `500` or hits a 2-second p99.9 tail stall *five hops downstream*, the head gateway already flipped `sampled = 00` 99% of the time—throwing away the exact forensic traces engineers desperately need!",
          "Stateful collectors inside `TailBasedSampler.collect_span` and `finalize_trace` solve head sampling's blindness by buffering all spans belonging to active traces briefly in stateful collector memory (`trace_id` sharded across OpenTelemetry collector rings!) and making the keep-vs-drop decision *only after the complete trace finishes arriving*! When `finalize_trace(trace_id)` inspects the completed span collection, it retains **100% of error traces (`has_error`)**, **100% of slow outlier traces (`duration_ms >= latency_threshold_ms`)**, plus a lightweight baseline `1%` sample of healthy fast traces for nominal profiling!",
          "Trace the operational scaling requirement forced cleanly onto distributed infrastructure by `TailBasedSampler`: because spans belonging to `trace_id = abc...` arrive scattered out of order from thirty independent microservices, **every collector tier must route all spans with the same `trace_id` to the exact same collector instance (`Consistent Hashing by trace_id`, Day 54!)**. If Span 1 lands on Collector A while Error Span 5 lands on Collector B, neither sees the full trace, breaking `finalize_trace` error correlation completely!",
